From: Salty <99386653+PjSalty@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 23:21:17 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 05/15] users: split the user query so sibling collections stop
 multiplying

Intent: UserQuery used AsSingleQuery over three SIBLING collections
(Permissions, Preferences, AccessSchedules), so EF emitted one query
that JOINed them and returned their cartesian product: ~24 permissions
x ~13 preferences made one user load ~312 rows, all 11 users 3,432.
AsSplitQuery is the documented remedy for this shape. Measured on a
live server: GetUserById 12.13ms -> 2.27ms per load (3 round trips
included), GetUsers 3,432 rows / 70.2ms -> 418 rows / 4.36ms; the two
statements were #1 and #2 by cumulative execution time because 5-19
user loads fire per grid page request. Results are unchanged: the
navigations are independent collections off one root. Carried
unchanged onto 12.x, where UserQuery is also the tracked load behind
RenameUser/UpdateUserAsync (split queries track and fix up the same
way) and EF 10 embeds the root's full ordering in each split statement.
Ceiling: the split statements are separate round trips outside a
transaction, so a concurrent write between them can hand back a user
row and collections from slightly different moments.
Drop when: upstream switches UserQuery to a split query.

Patch: 0007
---
 .../Users/UserManager.cs                         | 16 +++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Jellyfin.Server.Implementations/Users/UserManager.cs b/Jellyfin.Server.Implementations/Users/UserManager.cs
index b15f6b9..f3d7c33 100644
--- a/Jellyfin.Server.Implementations/Users/UserManager.cs
+++ b/Jellyfin.Server.Implementations/Users/UserManager.cs
@@ -134,8 +134,22 @@ namespace Jellyfin.Server.Implementations.Users
 
         private static IQueryable<User> UserQuery(JellyfinDbContext dbContext)
         {
+            // AsSplitQuery, not AsSingleQuery: Permissions, Preferences and
+            // AccessSchedules are three SIBLING collections, so a single query
+            // JOINs them together and returns their cartesian product. Every
+            // user carries ~24 permissions x ~13 preferences, so loading one
+            // user materialises ~312 rows of duplicated user columns to produce
+            // one User object, and loading all 11 users returned 3,432 rows.
+            //
+            // This is the documented EF Core remedy for exactly this shape
+            // (learn.microsoft.com "Single vs. split queries"). Measured on the
+            // live server: GetUserById 12.13ms -> 2.27ms per load (5.3x, three
+            // round trips included), GetUsers 3,432 rows in 70.2ms -> 418 rows
+            // in 4.36ms (16x). These two statements were #1 and #2 in the
+            // database by cumulative execution time (24,722s of the top-8's
+            // 40,000s), because 5-19 user loads fire per grid page request.
             return dbContext.Users
-                            .AsSingleQuery()
+                            .AsSplitQuery()
                             .Include(user => user.Permissions)
                             .Include(user => user.Preferences)
                             .Include(user => user.AccessSchedules)
